WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.754
In this video we see how Linux pairs each piece of hardware with the right driver, what probe really does, and how a driver shows itself to user space.

00:00:09.154 --> 00:00:13.775
Driver: Kernel code that knows how to operate one kind of hardware. Like: A translator who speaks one specific machine's language.

00:00:14.025 --> 00:00:19.725
probe(): The driver function the kernel calls when it finds a device that matches the driver. Like: The first handshake when a translator is introduced to the person they will translate for.

00:00:19.975 --> 00:00:27.080
User space: Where normal programs run, outside the kernel, protected from crashing the whole system. Like: The public area of a building, separate from the staff-only control room.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:35.352
A new board arrives. The HDMI bridge chip is soldered on, the driver is compiled in, but the screen stays black.

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:39.584
HDMI: The common cable standard for sending video and audio to TVs and monitors. Like: A garden hose for pictures and sound.

00:00:39.884 --> 00:00:42.264
dmesg shows nothing at all from the driver.

00:00:42.514 --> 00:00:47.344
Was it never matched to the hardware? Let's see how Linux pairs a device with its driver.

00:00:48.394 --> 00:00:50.379
Think of a wedding with a seating plan.

00:00:50.779 --> 00:00:55.748
Guests arrive wearing name tags. Those are devices, each with an identifier.

00:00:56.148 --> 00:01:02.894
Ushers read each tag. Those are the buses: platform, I two C, SPI, USB, PCI.

00:01:03.294 --> 00:01:14.648
I2C: A simple two-wire bus that lets a processor talk to small chips like sensors and HDMI bridges. Like: A classroom where the teacher calls each student by number and they answer one at a time.

00:01:14.948 --> 00:01:23.911
SPI: A fast four-wire bus for chips like flash memory and displays. Like: A dedicated phone line to each chip instead of a shared classroom.

00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:26.940
Each table has a host. Those are drivers.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:32.599
When an usher brings a guest to the right table, the host greets them. That greeting is the probe function.

00:01:32.999 --> 00:01:39.814
And if the host isn't ready yet, they say: come back later. In Linux, that's minus E PROBE DEFER.

00:01:41.014 --> 00:01:41.896
Some words.

00:01:42.296 --> 00:01:47.091
A module is a dot k o file you load into the running kernel with insmod or modprobe.

00:01:47.491 --> 00:01:54.213
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:01:54.463 --> 00:02:01.383
Probe is the driver function the kernel calls when a device matches. It turns the device on and registers its interfaces.

00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:10.630
Device managed, or devm, functions allocate resources that are freed automatically when the device is unbound. They remove whole classes of cleanup bugs.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:16.010
Sysfs is the slash sys filesystem, where kernel objects appear as small files.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:21.263
A character device is a node in slash dev that you read and write as a stream of bytes.

00:02:22.463 --> 00:02:25.145
Now the binding sequence, step by step.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:35.216
First, a device is registered. On ARM boards it usually comes from the device tree, with a compatible string, here poly comma h d m i bridge demo.

00:02:35.616 --> 00:02:48.445
Device tree: A text map of the hardware on a board (which chips, on which pins and addresses) that the kernel reads at boot. Like: The floor plan of a house, so the electrician knows where every socket is without digging into walls.

00:02:48.745 --> 00:02:53.598
Second, a driver registers, with a table of compatible strings it supports.

00:02:53.998 --> 00:02:58.979
Third, the bus core runs its match function. The strings are equal, so we have a pair.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:03.419
Fourth, the core calls the driver's probe function with the device.

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:12.225
Fifth, probe asks for its reset GPIO, but the GPIO controller driver hasn't loaded yet. So probe returns minus E PROBE DEFER.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:22.157
GPIO: General-purpose input/output: a single pin software can switch on or off, or read. Like: A light switch the software can flip or check.

00:03:22.457 --> 00:03:26.973
Sixth, the core parks the device on the deferred list. That's not an error yet.

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:33.573
Seventh, when the GPIO provider shows up, the core retries probe, and this time it returns zero.

00:03:33.973 --> 00:03:39.441
Eighth, the driver now creates its user facing interfaces: a dev node and sysfs attributes.

00:03:39.841 --> 00:03:45.124
sysfs / ioctl: Ways a program talks to a driver: sysfs files for simple settings, ioctl calls for commands. Like: Labelled switches on the wall (sysfs) and a request form at the counter (ioctl).

00:03:45.374 --> 00:03:49.820
Ninth, user space can open, read, write and send ioctls.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:55.339
Here's lab one. The write handler receives a pointer into user memory.

00:03:55.739 --> 00:03:59.315
We clamp the length, because user space can pass anything.

00:03:59.715 --> 00:04:04.289
A mutex protects the buffer, because two processes can write at the same time.

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:10.134
Copy from user can fault and sleep, which is why we use a mutex here and never a spinlock.

00:04:10.534 --> 00:04:19.149
Spinlock / mutex: Locks that stop two pieces of code changing the same data at once; a spinlock waits busily, a mutex sleeps. Like: A single bathroom key: a spinlock is waiting at the door, a mutex is taking a seat until called.

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:24.066
A read only sysfs attribute exposes the write counter, using sysfs emit.

00:04:24.466 --> 00:04:33.510
And a misc device gives us a dev node with almost no boilerplate. In the QEMU test, writing hello poly and reading it back worked, and the counter showed one.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:42.223
QEMU: A program that emulates a whole computer, so you can test kernels without real hardware. Like: A flight simulator for software.

00:04:43.323 --> 00:04:48.594
A very common bring up bug: the device simply never appears. Check five things.

00:04:48.994 --> 00:04:52.094
Is the device tree node enabled, with status okay?

00:04:52.494 --> 00:04:57.405
Does the compatible string match the driver's table exactly, character by character?

00:04:57.805 --> 00:05:02.774
Read devices deferred in debugfs. It lists every device waiting on a dependency.

00:05:03.174 --> 00:05:09.188
Search the kernel log for probe errors. Using dev err probe in your driver makes deferrals visible but quiet.

00:05:09.588 --> 00:05:12.688
And confirm the driver is actually built and loaded.

00:05:13.888 --> 00:05:20.215
Question for you: A driver is built in but its probe function never runs. Name two things to check first.

00:05:25.515 --> 00:05:36.069
Answer: Check that the device was actually created, for example in the device tree with the right compatible string, and that the driver's match table contains that same string.

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:37.865
Recap.

00:05:38.265 --> 00:05:42.178
A bus matches devices to drivers, and probe sets the device up.

00:05:42.578 --> 00:05:46.560
Minus E PROBE DEFER means wait for a dependency, not failure.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.073
Prefer device managed resources.

00:05:49.473 --> 00:05:54.756
Use existing frameworks like V four L two and ALSA before inventing custom ioctls.

00:05:55.156 --> 00:06:05.256
V4L2: Video for Linux 2: the kernel interface every Linux camera and video device uses. Like: The standard loading dock every camera truck backs into.

00:06:05.556 --> 00:06:17.480
ALSA / ASoC: Advanced Linux Sound Architecture: the kernel's audio system; ASoC is its part for chips on boards. Like: The plumbing that carries sound from microphones to programs and back to speakers.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.530
And never copy to or from user space while holding a spinlock.
